.TH EUCLID-CLI-ESS-CREATE-SECRET 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ess-create-secret \- store a new secret
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ess create-secret
.BR \-\-name | \-n
.I NAME
.RB ( \-\-value | \-v
.I TEXT
|
.BR \-\-value\-file | \-f
.I FILE
|
.BR \-\-value\-stdin )
[\fB\-\-description\fR|\fB\-d\fR \fITEXT\fR]
[\fB\-\-key\fR|\fB\-k\fR \fIERN\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Stores a secret \- a password, a connection string, a token, or JSON
holding several of those \- encrypted under an EKM key.
.PP
The value is encrypted before it is written and decrypted only by
.BR euclid-cli-ess-get-secret (1),
so a copy of the database yields nothing without
.BR euclid-cli-ekm (1).
The response carries the secret's metadata and never its value.
.PP
A name that already exists is refused rather than overwritten:
.BR euclid-cli-ess-update-secret (1)
is the action that means to replace a value, and a create that quietly
did it would be a way to destroy one by accident.
.SH GIVING THE VALUE
Three ways, and the obvious one is the worst. A value passed as
.B \-\-value
is visible in the process list for as long as the command runs and
stays in the shell history afterwards, which is a poor end for
something worth encrypting. Prefer
.B \-\-value\-file
or
.BR \-\-value\-stdin ,
which is what makes this composable:
.PP
.RS
.nf
openssl rand -base64 24 | euclid-cli ess create-secret \e
    --name db-password --value-stdin
.fi
.RE
.PP
Exactly one of the three must be given. A file or standard input has
one trailing newline removed \- an editor adds one that was never part
of the password \- and anything else is kept as it stands.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name " " \fINAME\fR
Name the secret is stored under, and the one an application asks for.
Unique within the caller's account and namespace. Required.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-value " " \fITEXT\fR
The secret itself. See
.B GIVING THE VALUE
above.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-value\-file " " \fIFILE\fR
Read the value from a file.
.TP
.B \-\-value\-stdin
Read the value from standard input.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-description " " \fITEXT\fR
What the secret is for. Free text, readable by anyone who may list
secrets \- so not the place for any part of the value. Optional.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " " \fIERN\fR
ERN of the EKM key to encrypt it under. Optional; without it the
namespace's own secrets key is used, which the module creates the first
time something needs it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The secret was stored; its metadata is printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
No value was given or it was given twice, the name is taken, the named
key does not exist or can no longer encrypt, or the server rejected the
request.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ess (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ess-get-secret (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ess-update-secret (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ess-list-secrets (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ess-delete-secret (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-create-key (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Jens Vogt <jens.vogt@opitz-consulting.com>
